As you can see, there're 3 instances of the service count-api. Each one of them has a service proxy. The exposed paths only map to the service that proxy is the sidecar for.

If we use this information to build targets for prometheus, those would be as follows:

```
target1 10.171.0.11:21932
target2: 10.171.0.20:24564
target3: 10.171.0.33:22253
```

Calling those targets `/metrics` path would lead us to:

```
target1 10.171.0.11:21932 -> "ServiceID": "_nomad-task-867b853c-f07f-ff53-743c-f01fdd47f784-group-api-count-api-9001"
target2: 10.171.0.20:24564 -> "ServiceID": "_nomad-task-8bad5753-0055-62e6-b46a-86d20722f374-group-api-count-api-9001"
target3: 10.171.0.33:22253 -> "ServiceID": "_nomad-task-1d5afc08-d773-dddb-99db-65b2611ddc47-group-api-count-api-9001"
```

Each target would map to one specific instance of the service. Those services are not reachable unless using those exposed paths (that's the reason of this PR).

If using consul connect to reach a service, that can take you to any instance of them, but this is different. The exposed paths, are not using the "connect protocol" they are just a mapping of ports where you can reach directly to the final service (usualy for metrics or healhchecks).

I hope I explained myself correctly. Thanks!
